# 4. Core Principles

## 4.1 Explainable

Every run must have an understandable reason.

Aura should be able to answer:

- What triggered this automation?
- Which conditions passed or failed?
- What actions were attempted?
- What succeeded?
- What failed?
- Why was a run skipped?

## 4.2 Deterministic

Given the same trigger event, state snapshot and automation version, evaluation should produce the same decision.

Time-sensitive or changing values must be captured in an evaluation snapshot.

## 4.3 Local First

Triggers, conditions and actions should be processed locally whenever platform capabilities permit.

Aura must not claim offline or background reliability that iOS cannot provide.

## 4.4 Safe by Default

Security-sensitive and destructive actions require stronger validation than ordinary lighting or media actions.

## 4.5 Idempotent Where Possible

Repeated delivery of the same trigger must not cause uncontrolled repeated execution.

## 4.6 User Control

Users can disable, edit, test, duplicate and delete their automations.

Automatic behavior must never remove the user’s ability to act manually.

## 4.7 Partial Failure

One failed action must not erase successful actions or make the execution history ambiguous.

